## Description

`loadSessions` is the single fetch path for the Sessions list. It is invoked from typing-driven search (`handleSearch`), filter changes (`handleFilterChange`), and pagination, but it applies its response unconditionally — there is no request-sequence guard, in-flight cancellation, or `AbortController`:

```tsx
// web/src/pages/Sessions.tsx:305-334
const loadSessions = useCallback(async (p: number, q?: string, ct?: string) => {
  setLoading(true);
  setError(null);
  try {
    const res = await api.listSessions(p, limit, { q: q || undefined, chatType: ct || undefined });
    setSessions(res.data.sessions);   // applied regardless of whether a newer request started
    setTotal(res.data.total);
    setPage(p);
  } catch (err) { /* ... */ } finally { setLoading(false); }
}, []);

const handleSearch = () => { loadSessions(1, searchQuery, chatTypeFilter); };
const handleFilterChange = (ct: string) => { setChatTypeFilter(ct); loadSessions(1, searchQuery, ct); };
```

If the user changes the query or filter (or paginates) while a previous `listSessions` call is still in flight, two requests race. Because each `await` resolves independently and writes directly into `setSessions`/`setTotal`/`setPage`, a slower **earlier** request that resolves last will overwrite the results of the **newer** request, leaving the displayed list out of sync with the current search/filter inputs.

## Impact

Under normal network jitter (a common condition on slower connections or a loaded backend), the Sessions table can display results for a stale query/filter/page while the input controls show the new criteria — a confusing, hard-to-reproduce correctness bug. The `total`/`page` state can likewise desynchronise, corrupting pagination. No data is lost, but the user is shown incorrect data; severity **medium**.

## Required Fix

1. Guard against out-of-order resolution: capture a monotonically increasing request id (or use an `AbortController` per call) and only call `setSessions`/`setTotal`/`setPage` if the resolved request is still the latest one.
2. Prefer `AbortController` so superseded requests are cancelled, and abort the in-flight request in a cleanup function when the component unmounts.
3. Add a test that fires two `loadSessions` calls and resolves them out of order, asserting the latest query's results win.

## References

- React docs — "You Might Not Need an Effect" / fetching data and ignoring stale responses: https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect#fetching-data

## Description

The two Server-Sent-Events streams (notifications and logs) open an `EventSource` without the `{ withCredentials: true }` option:

```ts
// web/src/lib/api.ts:1265-1267 (notifications)
connectNotifications(onCount: (count: number) => void) {
  const url = `${API_BASE}/notifications/stream`;
  const eventSource = new EventSource(url);          // no { withCredentials: true }

// web/src/lib/api.ts:1369-1371 (logs)
connectLogs(onLog: (entry: LogEntry) => void, onError?: (error: Event) => void) {
  const url = `${API_BASE}/logs/stream`;
  const eventSource = new EventSource(url);          // no { withCredentials: true }
```

The app authenticates with an HttpOnly session cookie, and every regular request in `fetchAPI` deliberately sets `credentials: "include"` (`web/src/lib/api.ts:612`) to carry that cookie. The two SSE connections do not. With the current `API_BASE = "/api"` (a same-origin relative path, `web/src/lib/api.ts:1`) this is harmless — `EventSource` sends same-origin cookies by default — but the moment the dashboard is served from a different origin than the API (a common reverse-proxy split, a separate API host, or a dev proxy), the streams will silently connect **without** the session cookie and the server will reject or drop them as unauthenticated.

## Impact

The defect is latent under the default same-origin deployment but breaks the live notification counter and live log stream in any cross-origin/split-host topology, with no error surfaced to the user beyond a silently dead stream. It is also an inconsistency with the rest of the client, which uniformly opts into credentialed requests. Severity is **low** (downgraded from the suggested medium: the streams do work in the default same-origin setup, so the original "streams fail" premise only holds cross-origin).

## Required Fix

1. Pass `{ withCredentials: true }` to both `new EventSource(url, { withCredentials: true })` calls so authenticated cookies are sent regardless of origin, mirroring `fetchAPI`'s `credentials: "include"`.
2. Ensure the SSE endpoints' CORS configuration returns `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` with a specific allowed origin when cross-origin serving is supported.

## References

- MDN — `EventSource()` `withCredentials`: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventSource/withCredentials

## Description

Two effects in `ProviderStep` call `onChange({ ...data, ... })` from inside async callbacks, but their dependency arrays omit `data`, so the spread `...data` closes over a **stale** snapshot of the prop:

```tsx
// web/src/components/setup/ProviderStep.tsx:30-46 (Claude Code key auto-detect)
useEffect(() => {
  if (selected?.autoDetectsKey) {
    setup.detectClaudeCodeKey()
      .then((result) => {
        if (result.found) onChange({ ...data, apiKey: '' });   // stale `data`
      })
      /* ... */;
  }
}, [selected?.id]);                                            // `data` not in deps

// web/src/components/setup/ProviderStep.tsx:49-64 (load models for provider)
useEffect(() => {
  /* ... */
  setup.getModels(data.provider).then((m) => {
    if (!data.model && m.length > 0) onChange({ ...data, model: m[0].value });  // stale `data`
  });
}, [data.provider]);                                           // only `data.provider` in deps
```

Because the wizard's `data` object is owned by the parent (`Setup`/`SetupContext`) and passed in as a prop, the closure captured when the effect ran holds the `data` value from that render. When the async `then` callback later fires `onChange({ ...data, model: ... })`, it merges into the **old** `data`, discarding any fields the user changed in the interim (e.g. an API key typed, or a model selected) and re-committing stale values to the parent.

## Impact

During setup, a user who edits another field while provider detection or model loading is still resolving can have those edits silently reverted when the async callback completes, because the stale-`data` spread overwrites the parent state. This is an intermittent, timing-dependent data-loss-of-edits bug in the first-run wizard. Severity **medium**.

## Required Fix

1. Avoid spreading a captured `data`: have `onChange` accept a functional updater (`onChange(prev => ({ ...prev, model: m[0].value }))`), or compute the patch with the latest value via a ref.
2. If the prop API cannot change, read `data` from a `useRef` kept in sync each render, and spread `dataRef.current` inside the async callbacks.
3. Add the lint-flagged dependencies (or document the ref-based access) so the stale-closure pattern cannot regress.

## References

- React docs — stale closures and effect dependencies: https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect#specifying-reactive-dependencies

## Description

After setup launches the agent, the dashboard auth token is handed off by embedding it directly in the URL query string:

```ts
// web/src/components/setup/SetupContext.tsx:240-248
const { token } = await setup.launch();
await setup.pollHealth(30000);
// Redirect to the dashboard with token-based auth
window.location.href = `/auth/exchange?token=${encodeURIComponent(token)}`;
```

The dashboard then reads that token back out of `window.location.search` and exchanges it for a session cookie:

```tsx
// web/src/App.tsx:51-65
const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
const exchangeToken = params.get("token");
if (window.location.pathname === "/auth/exchange" && exchangeToken) {
  login(exchangeToken).then((success) => {
    if (success) { window.location.href = "/"; }
    else { setLoading(false); setLoginError("Token exchange failed"); }
  });
  return;
}
```

Placing a credential in the query string means it is exposed everywhere URLs are recorded: the browser's history and address bar, the `Referer` header sent to any third-party resource loaded by `/auth/exchange`, proxy and web-server access logs, and any analytics/error-reporting that captures the full URL.

## Impact

The launch token is a bearer credential that is exchanged for an authenticated dashboard session. Leaking it via history/referrer/logs lets anyone with access to those sinks (a shared machine's browser history, a log aggregator, an intermediary proxy) replay the exchange and obtain a session, unless the token is strictly single-use and immediately invalidated. Even single-use tokens leak the fact and value of the credential into multiple persistence layers. Severity **medium**: exploitation requires access to one of those side channels, and the redirect to `/` after `login()` does not strip the token from history.

## Required Fix

1. Avoid the URL entirely: have `setup.launch()` set the session cookie server-side (or POST the token to `/auth/exchange` from JS) instead of redirecting with the token in the query string.
2. If a redirect handoff must be used, deliver the token in the URL **fragment** (`#token=...`, never sent to the server or logged) or a one-time POST, ensure the token is strictly single-use and short-TTL, and call `history.replaceState` to scrub it from the address bar/history immediately after exchange.

## References

- CWE-598: Use of GET Request Method With Sensitive Query Strings — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/598.html
- CWE-200: Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html
- OWASP — Information exposure through query strings in URL: https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Information_exposure_through_query_strings_in_url

## Description

The shared `fetchAPI` helper sends the HttpOnly session cookie on every request (`credentials: "include"`) but never reads the CSRF cookie or sets the matching CSRF request header for state-changing methods:

```ts
// web/src/lib/api.ts:603-621
async function fetchAPI<T>(endpoint: string, options?: RequestInit): Promise<T> {
  const headers: HeadersInit = {
    "Content-Type": "application/json",
    ...options?.headers,
  };
  const response = await fetch(`${API_BASE}${endpoint}`, {
    ...options,
    headers,
    credentials: "include", // send HttpOnly cookie automatically
  });
  /* ... */
  return response.json();
}
```

The backend implements the double-submit-cookie CSRF pattern and mounts it for all `/api/*` routes (`packages/api/src/server.ts:140` → `createCsrfMiddleware`), expecting browser clients to echo the `XSRF-TOKEN` cookie in an `X-CSRF-Token` header on mutating requests (`packages/api/src/middleware/csrf.middleware.ts:7,96-101`). The web client never participates: a repo-wide search of `web/src` finds no reference to `XSRF-TOKEN`, `X-CSRF-Token`, `csrf-token`, or `document.cookie`, and it never calls `GET /api/auth/csrf-token` to have the cookie issued. Because the middleware treats a request with **no** `XSRF-TOKEN` cookie as a non-browser client and lets it through (csrf.middleware.ts:92-94), the dashboard's mutations currently rely entirely on the session cookie's `SameSite` policy for CSRF defense rather than the intended token.

## Impact

The server's CSRF control is effectively dormant for the dashboard: every POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE issued through `fetchAPI` is authorised by the ambient session cookie alone, with no anti-CSRF token. The only remaining defense is the session cookie's `SameSite` attribute; if that is ever set to `Lax`/`None` (or bypassed), state-changing endpoints become cross-site-forgeable. Severity **low** given a correctly configured `SameSite` cookie mitigates most CSRF, but the layered control the codebase deliberately built is not actually in force.

## Required Fix

1. On app startup (post-login), call `GET /api/auth/csrf-token` so the `XSRF-TOKEN` cookie is issued.
2. In `fetchAPI`, for non-safe methods read the `XSRF-TOKEN` cookie and set the `X-CSRF-Token` header so it matches what `createCsrfMiddleware` validates.
3. Add a test asserting that a mutating `fetchAPI` call includes the `X-CSRF-Token` header equal to the cookie value.

## References

- CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/352.html
- OWASP — Cross-Site Request Forgery Prevention Cheat Sheet (double-submit cookie): https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross-Site_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html

## Description

The destructive delete handlers on the Hooks page call the delete API immediately on click, with no confirmation step:

```tsx
// web/src/pages/Hooks.tsx:129-136
const handleDeleteTrigger = async (id: string) => {
  try {
    await api.deleteTrigger(id);
    setTriggers((prev) => prev.filter((t) => t.id !== id));
  } catch (err) { setError(err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)); }
};

// web/src/pages/Hooks.tsx:197-204
const handleDeleteRule = async (id: string) => {
  try {
    await api.deleteRule(id);
    setRules((prev) => prev.filter((r) => r.id !== id));
  } catch (err) { setError(err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)); }
};
```

The application already ships a confirmation primitive — `App.tsx:136` wraps the authenticated routes in `<ConfirmDialogProvider>` — but neither delete handler uses it. A single misclick permanently deletes a trigger or structured rule, and there is no undo.

## Impact

Users can irreversibly delete hook triggers/rules with one accidental click, losing configuration that may be tedious to recreate. This is a usability/data-safety gap rather than a security issue; severity **low**.

## Required Fix

1. Wrap both deletions in the existing confirm dialog (`useConfirm()` from `ConfirmDialogProvider`), gating `api.deleteTrigger`/`api.deleteRule` on an explicit user confirmation that names the item being deleted.
2. Apply the same confirmation to any other destructive actions on the page for consistency.

## References

- NN/g — Confirmation dialogs for destructive actions: https://www.nngroup.com/articles/confirmation-dialog/

## Description

When the provider changes, the model-loading effect silently writes a new model value straight to the server via `saveConfig`, with no debounce and no explicit user save:

```ts
// web/src/hooks/useConfigState.ts:96-109
const currentProvider = getLocal('agent.provider');
useEffect(() => {
  if (!currentProvider) return;
  api.getModelsForProvider(currentProvider).then((res) => {
    const models = res.data.map((m) => ({ value: m.value, name: m.name }));
    setModelOptions(models);
    const currentModel = localInputs['agent.model'] ?? '';
    if (models.length > 0 && !models.some((m) => m.value === currentModel)) {
      saveConfig('agent.model', models[0].value);   // immediate PUT to the server
    }
  }).catch(() => setModelOptions([]));
}, [currentProvider]);
```

`saveConfig` (`useConfigState.ts:70-80`) immediately issues `api.setConfigKey(...)` and reloads. So merely switching the provider — whenever the previously-selected model isn't in the new provider's list — persists `agent.model = models[0].value` to the backend as a side effect, rather than staging it for the user to review and explicitly save like other fields.

## Impact

A configuration change (the agent's model) is committed to the server implicitly as a side effect of an unrelated UI interaction, bypassing the page's explicit save/cancel flow. The user has no opportunity to review or cancel, the change can be surprising, and the extra unconditional PUT runs on every qualifying provider switch. Severity **low** — it is a correctness/UX concern (the original "on every change" wording is slightly broad; it fires whenever the current model falls outside the new provider's list).

## Required Fix

1. Stage the auto-selected model in local state (`setLocal('agent.model', ...)`) and let the existing explicit save action persist it, rather than calling `saveConfig` from the effect.
2. If auto-persistence is intended, debounce it and surface the implicit save to the user (e.g. a toast) so it is not silent.

## References

- React docs — "You Might Not Need an Effect" (avoid side effects like network writes in render-driven effects): https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect

## Description

The top-level `ErrorBoundary` (wrapping the entire app in `App.tsx:31,135`) implements only `getDerivedStateFromError` to render a fallback. It has no `componentDidCatch`, and it never logs the error or its React component stack anywhere:

```tsx
// web/src/components/ErrorBoundary.tsx:12-33
export class ErrorBoundary extends Component<Props, State> {
  state: State = { hasError: false, error: null };

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error: Error): State {
    return { hasError: true, error: error.message };
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      return (
        <div className="card" /* ... */>
          <h2 /* ... */>Something went wrong</h2>
          <p /* ... */>{this.state.error}</p>
          <button onClick={() => this.setState({ hasError: false, error: null })}>Try Again</button>
        </div>
      );
    }
    return this.props.children;
  }
}
```

The caught error's stack and the React `componentStack` (available only via `componentDidCatch(error, info)`) are discarded — not sent to any telemetry sink and not even written to `console.error`.

## Impact

When a render-time exception trips the boundary in production, the team gets no signal: no stack trace, no component stack, no breadcrumb. The user sees a generic "Something went wrong" with `error.message` only, and the actual fault is unobservable, making such crashes effectively undiagnosable from the field. Severity **low** (it correctly prevents a white-screen crash; the gap is observability).

## Required Fix

1. Add `componentDidCatch(error, info)` and report `error` plus `info.componentStack` to the project's logging/telemetry channel (at minimum `console.error`, ideally forwarded to the existing log/notification backend).
2. Optionally include a correlation id in the fallback UI so users can reference the reported error.

## References

- React docs — Error boundaries and `componentDidCatch`: https://react.dev/reference/react/Component#componentdidcatch

## Description

`loadSaved` parses persisted dashboard layout from `localStorage` and casts the result straight to `SavedState`, then feeds it into component state and the grid renderer:

```ts
// web/src/components/widgets/DashboardGrid.tsx:128-136
function loadSaved(): SavedState | null {
  try {
    const raw = localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
    if (raw) return JSON.parse(raw) as SavedState;   // cast only — no shape validation
  } catch {
    // ignore
  }
  return null;
}

// web/src/components/widgets/DashboardGrid.tsx:179-185
const saved = loadSaved();
const [layouts, setLayouts] = useState<ResponsiveLayouts>(saved?.layouts ?? buildDefaultLayouts(ALL_VISIBLE));
const [visible, setVisible]  = useState<WidgetId[]>(saved?.visible ?? ALL_VISIBLE);
```

The `try/catch` correctly handles **invalid JSON** (a parse exception falls through to `return null`). What it does not handle is **structurally valid but wrong-shaped** JSON: a value like `{"layouts": 123}` or `{"visible": "x"}` parses cleanly, satisfies the unchecked `as SavedState` cast, and is then passed as `layouts`/`visible` into `react-grid-layout`, which expects arrays of layout items. Malformed shapes can throw during render (uncaught by `loadSaved`) and white-screen the dashboard.

## Impact

A tampered or stale/incompatible `localStorage` entry (e.g. left over from a previous layout schema, or hand-edited) can crash the entire Dashboard render even though JSON parsing succeeded, with no recovery path other than clearing storage. Because `loadSaved` is invoked during render of `InnerGrid`, the throw escapes to the surrounding `ErrorBoundary`. Severity **low** — it requires locally-modified or schema-drifted storage. (Note: the suggested premise "crash on tampered data" is partly inaccurate — JSON parse failures *are* caught; the unhandled case is valid JSON of the wrong shape.)

## Required Fix

1. Validate the parsed object before use: check that `layouts.lg`/`layouts.md` are arrays of well-formed layout items and `visible` is an array of known `WidgetId`s; on mismatch, discard and fall back to defaults.
2. Version the stored schema (a `version` field) and drop entries from older/unknown versions, mirroring the import-bundle versioning used elsewhere.

## References

- OWASP — Client-side storage and the need to validate untrusted persisted data: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTML5_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html#local-storage

## Description

The monthly-budget save handler parses the text input with `parseFloat` and silently maps anything non-numeric to `null`:

```tsx
// web/src/pages/Analytics.tsx:564-575
const handleSaveBudget = async () => {
  setSaving(true);
  try {
    const val = limitInput.trim() === "" ? null : parseFloat(limitInput);
    const res = await api.setAnalyticsBudget(isNaN(val as number) ? null : val);
    setBudget(res.data ?? null);
  } catch (e) {
    setError(e instanceof Error ? e.message : "Failed to save budget");
  } finally {
    setSaving(false);
  }
};
```

Three problematic coercions occur with no user feedback: a non-numeric entry like `"abc"` becomes `NaN` → coerced to `null` (clears the budget); a partially-numeric entry like `"12abc"` is silently accepted by `parseFloat` as `12`; and a negative value like `-50` passes straight through as a negative budget. The user receives no validation error in any of these cases — the save appears to succeed while doing something other than what was typed.

## Impact

A mistyped budget can silently **clear** the configured monthly limit (disabling budget enforcement) or persist an unintended value (a truncated or negative number), with the UI giving no indication the input was invalid. For a cost-control feature this is a meaningful correctness/UX gap. Severity **low**.

## Required Fix

1. Validate before saving: reject `NaN`, reject negatives, and require the full string to be numeric (e.g. `Number(limitInput)` plus a numeric regex) rather than tolerant `parseFloat`.
2. Show an inline validation error for invalid input, and only treat an explicitly-empty field as the intentional "clear budget" (`null`) case.

## References

- CWE-20: Improper Input Validation — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html

## Description

The configuration importer reads a user-selected file, `JSON.parse`s it with an unchecked cast to `ConfigBundle`, and validates only the `version` field before sending the whole object to the server:

```tsx
// web/src/components/ExportImportPanel.tsx:32-55
const handleImportFile = async (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
  const file = e.target.files?.[0];
  if (!file) return;
  setImporting(true);
  try {
    const text = await file.text();
    const bundle = JSON.parse(text) as ConfigBundle;       // cast only

    if (!bundle.version || bundle.version !== '1.0') {       // version is the ONLY guard
      toast.error('Invalid configuration bundle format');
      return;
    }
    const res = await api.importConfig(bundle, importOptions);
    toast.success(`Configuration imported: ${res.data.applied.join(', ')}`);
  } catch (err) {
    toast.error(`Import failed: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`);
  } finally {
    setImporting(false);
    if (fileRef.current) fileRef.current.value = '';
  }
};
```

Any file whose top-level `version` equals `'1.0'` is accepted and forwarded to `api.importConfig` regardless of the shape or contents of its `config`/`hooks`/`soul` sections. There is no schema validation of the payload itself, so an arbitrarily-structured object (correct version tag, wrong/garbage body) is pushed to the backend.

## Impact

A malformed or hand-crafted bundle that merely carries `version: '1.0'` is sent to the import endpoint unchecked, shifting all shape-validation responsibility to the server and risking partial/garbage configuration being applied if the server is equally permissive. The blast radius depends on backend validation; from the client side this is a low-severity robustness gap. (The suggested "no schema guard" wording is slightly strong — a minimal version-tag guard exists; the payload body is what is unvalidated.)

## Required Fix

1. Validate the bundle structure client-side before import — confirm the presence/type of each enabled section (`config`, `hooks`, `soul`) and reject unknown top-level keys — ideally with a shared schema (e.g. Zod) reused by export and the backend.
2. Surface a specific error identifying which section failed validation rather than a generic "Invalid configuration bundle format".

## References

- CWE-20: Improper Input Validation — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html
- OWASP — Mass assignment / unvalidated input deserialization: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Mass_Assignment_Cheat_Sheet.html
